@@ -252,4 +252,4 @@ The Colossus-style bootstrap recursion bottoms out here: etcd depends on nothing
 
 The dependency rule (ADR-0010): implementations and consumers depend on `traits`, never on each other's concretes. Only `server` knows the concrete backends, which is what makes "swap redb for FoundationDB" or "in-memory for etcd" a composition change rather than a refactor. The seam earned its keep in M4: swapping the production backend from TiKV to FoundationDB (ADR-0042) touched the `server` wiring and added a crate, and changed no consumer.
 
-`validate` sits outside that rule on purpose: it is a client of a running deployment, not a part of one, and depends on no `wyrd-*` crate (proposal 0017 §9). Its command-line surface is ten required flags — `--endpoint`, `--region`, `--bucket`, `--scenario`, `--duration`, `--workers`, `--seed`, `--out`, `--run-id`, `--driver-placement` — with S3 credentials taken from `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, else `WYRD_S3_ACCESS_KEY`/`WYRD_S3_SECRET_KEY`, else refused; half a pair, or a variable whose value is not UTF-8, is refused by name rather than skipped over to the next pair (an empty variable counts as unset). Parsing is strict, unlike `wyrd`'s: an unknown flag, a repeated flag, an empty value, a stray positional, or a `--`-prefixed token in a value slot is refused by name rather than absorbed. The library carries the S3 client every scenario calls through (#852): `aws-sdk-s3` on the `--endpoint`, path-style, with static SigV4 credentials, plain HTTP (TLS is terminated in front of the gateway), and retries and stalled-stream protection off. Its error is typed: HTTP status, `<Code>`, `<Message>`, and the `x-amz-request-id` read from that header itself. Connect, operation, and body-idle deadlines bound every wait, and object bodies stream both ways, so what the client holds of an object does not grow with its size. The AWS SDK is the only shipped dependency `validate` adds; Wyrd crates appear only as dev-dependencies, for the in-process gateway its tests run against. The binary still echoes its resolved configuration (the access-key id, never the secret) and exits without issuing a request; scenarios arrive with #743.
+`validate` sits outside that rule on purpose: it is a client of a running deployment, not a part of one, and depends on no `wyrd-*` crate (proposal 0017 §9). Its command-line surface is ten required flags — `--endpoint`, `--region`, `--bucket`, `--scenario`, `--duration`, `--workers`, `--seed`, `--out`, `--run-id`, `--driver-placement` — with S3 credentials taken from `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, else `WYRD_S3_ACCESS_KEY`/`WYRD_S3_SECRET_KEY`, else refused; half a pair, or a variable whose value is not UTF-8, is refused by name rather than skipped over to the next pair (an empty variable counts as unset). Parsing is strict, unlike `wyrd`'s: an unknown flag, a repeated flag, an empty value, a stray positional, or a `--`-prefixed token in a value slot is refused by name rather than absorbed. The library carries the S3 client every scenario calls through (#852): `aws-sdk-s3` on the `--endpoint`, path-style, with static SigV4 credentials, plain HTTP (TLS is terminated in front of the gateway), and retries and stalled-stream protection off. Its error is typed: HTTP status, `<Code>`, `<Message>`, and the `x-amz-request-id` read from that header itself. A response that is not a whole, well-formed, correctly framed S3 response is never reported as one (#853): the client reads an error body itself rather than through the SDK — empty is "no body", an `<Error>` without `<Code>` is "no code", and anything but one whole `<Error>` document is unreadable — refuses framing that cannot mark where a body ends (both `Transfer-Encoding` and `Content-Length`, or, for a GET object, neither), and reports a success the SDK cannot read as unreadable with the SDK's diagnostic. Every body it buffers (every error response, every PUT or DELETE response) is capped by a fixed 64 KiB budget, counted as the body arrives, so a body with no declared length is cut off rather than read whole. Connect, operation, and body-idle deadlines bound every wait, and object bodies stream both ways, so what the client holds of an object does not grow with its size; a GET object streams outside the budget, framed by its `Content-Length` or by complete chunked framing. The AWS SDK, and `roxmltree` for reading error documents, are the only shipped dependencies `validate` adds; Wyrd crates appear only as dev-dependencies, for the in-process gateway its tests run against. The binary still echoes its resolved configuration (the access-key id, never the secret) and exits without issuing a request; scenarios arrive with #743.
